.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/type-check Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "type-check [![Build Status](https://travis-ci.org/gkz/type-check.png?branch=master)](https://travis-ci.org/gkz/type-check)"
.PP

.PP
\fRtype-check\fP is a library which allows you to check the types of JavaScript values at runtime with a Haskell like type syntax\&. It is great for checking external input, for testing, or even for adding a bit of safety to your internal code\&. It is a major component of \fRlevn\fP\&. MIT license\&. Version 0\&.4\&.0\&. Check out the \fRdemo\fP\&.

.PP
For updates on \fRtype-check\fP, \fRfollow me on twitter\fP\&. 
.PP
.nf
npm install type-check

.fi
.PP
.SH "Quick Examples"
.PP
.PP
.nf
// Basic types:
var typeCheck = require('type\-check')\&.typeCheck;
typeCheck('Number', 1);               // true
typeCheck('Number', 'str');           // false
typeCheck('Error', new Error);        // true
typeCheck('Undefined', undefined);    // true

// Comment
typeCheck('count::Number', 1);        // true

// One type OR another type:
typeCheck('Number | String', 2);      // true
typeCheck('Number | String', 'str');  // true

// Wildcard, matches all types:
typeCheck('*', 2) // true

// Array, all elements of a single type:
typeCheck('[Number]', [1, 2, 3]);                // true
typeCheck('[Number]', [1, 'str', 3]);            // false

// Tuples, or fixed length arrays with elements of different types:
typeCheck('(String, Number)', ['str', 2]);       // true
typeCheck('(String, Number)', ['str']);          // false
typeCheck('(String, Number)', ['str', 2, 5]);    // false

// Object properties:
typeCheck('{x: Number, y: Boolean}', {x: 2, y: false});             // true
typeCheck('{x: Number, y: Boolean}',       {x: 2});                 // false
typeCheck('{x: Number, y: Maybe Boolean}', {x: 2});                 // true
typeCheck('{x: Number, y: Boolean}',      {x: 2, y: false, z: 3});  // false
typeCheck('{x: Number, y: Boolean, \&.\&.\&.}', {x: 2, y: false, z: 3});  // true

// A particular type AND object properties:
typeCheck('RegExp{source: String, \&.\&.\&.}', /re/i);          // true
typeCheck('RegExp{source: String, \&.\&.\&.}', {source: 're'}); // false

// Custom types:
var opt = {customTypes:
  {Even: { typeOf: 'Number', validate: function(x) { return x % 2 === 0; }}}};
typeCheck('Even', 2, opt); // true

// Nested:
var type = '{a: (String, [Number], {y: Array, \&.\&.\&.}), b: Error{message: String, \&.\&.\&.}}'
typeCheck(type, {a: ['hi', [1, 2, 3], {y: [1, 'ms']}], b: new Error('oh no')}); // true
.fi
.PP

.PP
Check out the \fBtype syntax format\fP and \fBguide\fP\&.
.SH "Usage"
.PP
\fRrequire('type-check');\fP returns an object that exposes four properties\&. \fRVERSION\fP is the current version of the library as a string\&. \fRtypeCheck\fP, \fRparseType\fP, and \fRparsedTypeCheck\fP are functions\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
// typeCheck(type, input, options);
typeCheck('Number', 2);               // true

// parseType(type);
var parsedType = parseType('Number'); // object

// parsedTypeCheck(parsedType, input, options);
parsedTypeCheck(parsedType, 2);       // true
.fi
.PP
.SS "typeCheck(type, input, options)"
\fRtypeCheck\fP checks a JavaScript value \fRinput\fP against \fRtype\fP written in the \fBtype format\fP (and taking account the optional \fRoptions\fP) and returns whether the \fRinput\fP matches the \fRtype\fP\&.
.SS "arguments"
.IP "\(bu" 2
type - \fRString\fP - the type written in the \fBtype format\fP which to check against
.IP "\(bu" 2
input - \fR*\fP - any JavaScript value, which is to be checked against the type
.IP "\(bu" 2
options - \fRMaybe Object\fP - an optional parameter specifying additional options, currently the only available option is specifying \fBcustom types\fP
.PP
.SS "returns"
\fRBoolean\fP - whether the input matches the type
.SS "example"
.PP
.nf
typeCheck('Number', 2); // true
.fi
.PP
.SS "parseType(type)"
\fRparseType\fP parses string \fRtype\fP written in the \fBtype format\fP into an object representing the parsed type\&.
.SS "arguments"
.IP "\(bu" 2
type - \fRString\fP - the type written in the \fBtype format\fP which to parse
.PP
.SS "returns"
\fRObject\fP - an object in the parsed type format representing the parsed type
.SS "example"
.PP
.nf
parseType('Number'); // [{type: 'Number'}]
.fi
.PP
 
.SS "parsedTypeCheck(parsedType, input, options)"
\fRparsedTypeCheck\fP checks a JavaScript value \fRinput\fP against parsed \fRtype\fP in the parsed type format (and taking account the optional \fRoptions\fP) and returns whether the \fRinput\fP matches the \fRtype\fP\&. Use this in conjunction with \fRparseType\fP if you are going to use a type more than once\&.
.SS "arguments"
.IP "\(bu" 2
type - \fRObject\fP - the type in the parsed type format which to check against
.IP "\(bu" 2
input - \fR*\fP - any JavaScript value, which is to be checked against the type
.IP "\(bu" 2
options - \fRMaybe Object\fP - an optional parameter specifying additional options, currently the only available option is specifying \fBcustom types\fP
.PP
.SS "returns"
\fRBoolean\fP - whether the input matches the type
.SS "example"
.PP
.nf
parsedTypeCheck([{type: 'Number'}], 2); // true
var parsedType = parseType('String');
parsedTypeCheck(parsedType, 'str');     // true
.fi
.PP

.PP
 
.SH "Type Format"
.PP
.SS "Syntax"
White space is ignored\&. The root node is a \fBTypes\fP\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBIdentifier\fP = \fR[\\$\\w]+\fP - a group of any lower or upper case letters, numbers, underscores, or dollar signs - eg\&. \fRString\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBType\fP = an \fRIdentifier\fP, an \fRIdentifier\fP followed by a \fRStructure\fP, just a \fRStructure\fP, or a wildcard \fR*\fP - eg\&. \fRString\fP, \fRObject{x: Number}\fP, \fR{x: Number}\fP, \fRArray{0: String, 1: Boolean, length: Number}\fP, \fR*\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBTypes\fP = optionally a comment (an \fRIdentifier\fP followed by a \fR::\fP), optionally the identifier \fRMaybe\fP, one or more \fRType\fP, separated by \fR|\fP - eg\&. \fRNumber\fP, \fRString | Date\fP, \fRMaybe Number\fP, \fRMaybe Boolean | String\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBStructure\fP = \fRFields\fP, or a \fRTuple\fP, or an \fRArray\fP - eg\&. \fR{x: Number}\fP, \fR(String, Number)\fP, \fR[Date]\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBFields\fP = a \fR{\fP, followed one or more \fRField\fP separated by a comma \fR,\fP (trailing comma \fR,\fP is permitted), optionally an \fR\&.\&.\&.\fP (always preceded by a comma \fR,\fP), followed by a \fR}\fP - eg\&. \fR{x: Number, y: String}\fP, \fR{k: Function, \&.\&.\&.}\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBField\fP = an \fRIdentifier\fP, followed by a colon \fR:\fP, followed by \fRTypes\fP - eg\&. \fRx: Date | String\fP, \fRy: Boolean\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBTuple\fP = a \fR(\fP, followed by one or more \fRTypes\fP separated by a comma \fR,\fP (trailing comma \fR,\fP is permitted), followed by a \fR)\fP - eg \fR(Date)\fP, \fR(Number, Date)\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBArray\fP = a \fR[\fP followed by exactly one \fRTypes\fP followed by a \fR]\fP - eg\&. \fR[Boolean]\fP, \fR[Boolean | Null]\fP
.PP
.SS "Guide"
\fRtype-check\fP uses \fRObject\&.toString\fP to find out the basic type of a value\&. Specifically,

.PP
.PP
.nf
{}\&.toString\&.call(VALUE)\&.slice(8, \-1)
{}\&.toString\&.call(true)\&.slice(8, \-1) // 'Boolean'
.fi
.PP
 A basic type, eg\&. \fRNumber\fP, uses this check\&. This is much more versatile than using \fRtypeof\fP - for example, with \fRdocument\fP, \fRtypeof\fP produces \fR'object'\fP which isn't that useful, and our technique produces \fR'HTMLDocument'\fP\&.

.PP
You may check for multiple types by separating types with a \fR|\fP\&. The checker proceeds from left to right, and passes if the value is any of the types - eg\&. \fRString | Boolean\fP first checks if the value is a string, and then if it is a boolean\&. If it is none of those, then it returns false\&.

.PP
Adding a \fRMaybe\fP in front of a list of multiple types is the same as also checking for \fRNull\fP and \fRUndefined\fP - eg\&. \fRMaybe String\fP is equivalent to \fRUndefined | Null | String\fP\&.

.PP
You may add a comment to remind you of what the type is for by following an identifier with a \fR::\fP before a type (or multiple types)\&. The comment is simply thrown out\&.

.PP
The wildcard \fR*\fP matches all types\&.

.PP
There are three types of structures for checking the contents of a value: 'fields', 'tuple', and 'array'\&.

.PP
If used by itself, a 'fields' structure will pass with any type of object as long as it is an instance of \fRObject\fP and the properties pass - this allows for duck typing - eg\&. \fR{x: Boolean}\fP\&.

.PP
To check if the properties pass, and the value is of a certain type, you can specify the type - eg\&. \fRError{message: String}\fP\&.

.PP
If you want to make a field optional, you can simply use \fRMaybe\fP - eg\&. \fR{x: Boolean, y: Maybe String}\fP will still pass if \fRy\fP is undefined (or null)\&.

.PP
If you don't care if the value has properties beyond what you have specified, you can use the 'etc' operator \fR\&.\&.\&.\fP - eg\&. \fR{x: Boolean, \&.\&.\&.}\fP will match an object with an \fRx\fP property that is a boolean, and with zero or more other properties\&.

.PP
For an array, you must specify one or more types (separated by \fR|\fP) - it will pass for something of any length as long as each element passes the types provided - eg\&. \fR[Number]\fP, \fR[Number | String]\fP\&.

.PP
A tuple checks for a fixed number of elements, each of a potentially different type\&. Each element is separated by a comma - eg\&. \fR(String, Number)\fP\&.

.PP
An array and tuple structure check that the value is of type \fRArray\fP by default, but if another type is specified, they will check for that instead - eg\&. \fRInt32Array[Number]\fP\&. You can use the wildcard \fR*\fP to search for any type at all\&.

.PP
Check out the \fRtype precedence\fP library for type-check\&.
.SH "Options"
.PP
Options is an object\&. It is an optional parameter to the \fRtypeCheck\fP and \fRparsedTypeCheck\fP functions\&. The only current option is \fRcustomTypes\fP\&.

.PP
 
.SS "Custom Types"
\fBExample:\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
var options = {
  customTypes: {
    Even: {
      typeOf: 'Number',
      validate: function(x) {
        return x % 2 === 0;
      }
    }
  }
};
typeCheck('Even', 2, options); // true
typeCheck('Even', 3, options); // false
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fRcustomTypes\fP allows you to set up custom types for validation\&. The value of this is an object\&. The keys of the object are the types you will be matching\&. Each value of the object will be an object having a \fRtypeOf\fP property - a string, and \fRvalidate\fP property - a function\&.

.PP
The \fRtypeOf\fP property is the type the value should be (optional - if not set only \fRvalidate\fP will be used), and \fRvalidate\fP is a function which should return true if the value is of that type\&. \fRvalidate\fP receives one parameter, which is the value that we are checking\&.
.SH "Technical About"
.PP
\fRtype-check\fP is written in \fRLiveScript\fP - a language that compiles to JavaScript\&. It also uses the \fRprelude\&.ls\fP library\&. 
